            "content": "## Why\n\nChat retrieval is lexical only. The recorded baseline (`apps/api/src/search/chat/eval/BASELINE.md`) measures Recall@10 = 1.00 on exact-title, exact-content, code, and typo queries — and **0.00 with a 1.00 zero-result rate on paraphrase**, because a query that shares no tokens with the chat cannot be reached by FTS or trigram at all. The same baseline records that its Russian/Spanish rows score 1.00 only because the fixture queries reuse surface word forms, and that `simple` FTS performs no stemming, so inflected Russian will regress on real multilingual data.\n\nClosing that gap needs a vector leg, and a vector leg needs storage, a provider boundary, and a backfilled corpus before any query can use it. This change lands that substrate **without touching the query path**, so the async and provider machinery bakes in production while search behavior stays exactly as it is today. Flipping retrieval on is a separate, separately reviewable change.\n\n## What Changes\n\n- **BREAKING (deployment):** Postgres must provide the `vector` extension. `compose.yaml`, `scripts/rls-test.sh`, and CI move off `postgres:17-alpine` to a digest-pinned pgvector image; pgvector joins `pg_trgm` as a documented self-host requirement in README and AGENTS. `pg_trgm` shipped in the base image and needed no swap; `vector` does not.\n- A **global embedding-model registry** keyed by a stable internal key, carrying provider, provider model, revision, dimensions, distance metric, optional asymmetric document/query prefixes, and enablement flags. Application code references internal keys only; provider names never leak past the adapter. **Which model serves queries is per-corpus configuration**, not one global flip, so corpora that backfill at different speeds cannot strand each other.\n- A **per-corpus embedding store** for chat chunks, holding one vector per (document, model) so model generations coexist, with denormalized owner and chat columns and RLS `ENABLE` + `FORCE` and no public-read — the same isolation invariants the lexical projection already carries.\n- A **content-hash validity rule**: an embedding is valid only while its recorded content hash still matches the projection row it describes. Results that arrive after the underlying content was edited or deleted are dropped, never applied, so a late provider response can never restore deleted content.\n- A **provider-neutral embedding backend** behind one interface, with an OpenAI-compatible adapter that covers both self-hosted (Ollama) and cloud endpoints.\n- An **asynchronous embedding pipeline** on the existing queue: the phase-1 reindex path additionally enqueues missing or stale embeddings for the active model, and a dedicated worker embeds, validates, and persists them. Lexical freshness is unaffected; embedding freshness is allowed to lag.\n- A **backfill command** for the existing corpus, idempotent by content hash, plus per-chat indexed-at and embedded-at observability.\n- **Fail-closed degrade**: with no embedding configuration, the instance runs clean and search stays lexical. Absent configuration is never an error path.\n- **Model-migration discipline**: register a new model, backfill in parallel, evaluate against the recorded eval baseline, activate, retire the old vectors after a safe period. Vectors are never overwritten in place; a provider change is a new internal key.\n- **No query-path change.** `ChatsRepository.searchByOwner`, its ranking, snippets, and output contract are untouched, and the eval baseline is expected to be byte-identical after this change.\n\n## Capabilities\n\n### New Capabilities\n\n- `search-embeddings`: the derived vector layer over the search projection — model registry and per-corpus active-model selection, per-corpus vector storage and its tenant-isolation invariants, the content-hash validity rule, the provider-neutral backend boundary, asynchronous embedding production and backfill, fail-closed degrade when unconfigured, and model-migration discipline. Like the lexical projection, it is derived state that is fully rebuildable from canonical `chats`/`messages`.\n\n### Modified Capabilities\n\n- `search-projection`: the reindex path gains a second consumer — it additionally enqueues embedding work for the active model — and the writer partition between the two tiers becomes a stated invariant (the lexical tier owns content columns, the embedding tier owns only vectors). Deletion propagation extends to derived vectors, and `vector` joins `pg_trgm` as a declared platform dependency.\n- `instance-config`: an optional embedding-model section is added to `llame.config.json` under the existing schema-as-boot-validator and `{env:...}` / `{path:...}` interpolation rules. Embedding selection is operator config because background indexing is instance-scoped rather than per-request; absent configuration degrades to lexical rather than failing boot.\n\n`chat-search` is deliberately **not** modified. Its matching semantics, fused ranking, output contract, and eval baseline are the subject of the follow-on hybrid-retrieval change.\n\n## Impact\n\n- **Deployment:** `compose.yaml`, `scripts/rls-test.sh`, CI Postgres services, README and AGENTS self-host requirements. Existing self-hosters must move to a pgvector-capable image before upgrading.\n- **Schema:** new migration adding the model registry and the chat-chunk vector store, both under FORCE RLS with cross-tenant negatives added to `scripts/rls-test.sh`.\n- **Code:** `apps/api/src/search/core/` gains the corpus-agnostic backend interface and adapters; a chat-scoped embedding worker joins the existing reindex worker and queue definitions in `apps/api/src/search/`.\n- **Operations:** a new queue and worker to observe, a backfill to run once per existing instance, and one new outbound provider dependency when configured.\n- **Cost:** embedding an existing corpus is a one-time provider spend proportional to chunk count, and ongoing spend proportional to new turns. A self-hosted backend makes both zero.\n- **Not affected:** the command palette, `search_conversations`, ranking, snippets, and every existing search test — this change is intended to be invisible to users until the follow-on retrieval change lands.\n",
