            "content": "## Purpose\n\nThe **embedding layer** is a derived, fully rebuildable vector projection over the lexical search projection: one embedding per (document chunk, embedding model), produced asynchronously through a provider-neutral backend and valid only while the content it was derived from is unchanged. It covers the operator-declared embedding-model registry and per-corpus active-model selection, per-corpus vector storage and its datastore-enforced tenant isolation, the content-hash validity rule that keeps stale and deleted content unrecoverable, asynchronous production and backfill, fail-closed degrade when no model is configured, and the discipline for migrating between models. It produces vectors only; the query path does not read them in this capability.\n\n## ADDED Requirements\n\n### Requirement: The embedding layer is derived state that never changes search behavior\n\nEmbeddings SHALL be derived from the lexical search projection, which is itself derived from canonical `chats`/`messages`. The embedding layer MUST be fully rebuildable at any time from that canonical content, and MUST NOT modify canonical tables or the lexical projection's content. Retrieval behavior — matching semantics, ranking, snippets, and result contract — SHALL be identical with and without embeddings present; no query path reads a vector in this capability.\n\n#### Scenario: Search results are unchanged by the presence of embeddings\n\n- **WHEN** the recorded relevance eval runs on an instance with a fully embedded corpus and again on one with no embeddings at all\n- **THEN** both runs produce the same ranked results and the same recorded metrics\n\n#### Scenario: Full rebuild reproduces the vector layer\n\n- **WHEN** all embeddings are deleted and the backfill mechanism runs to completion\n- **THEN** every projection document that the active model covers has an embedding again, and no canonical or lexical-projection row was modified\n\n### Requirement: Embedding models are registered under stable internal keys\n\nEmbedding models SHALL be registered with a stable internal key that application code and stored vectors reference. A registry entry SHALL record its provider connection, provider-side model identifier, model revision, vector dimensions, distance metric, optional asymmetric document and query prefixes, and whether it is enabled. Provider-side identifiers SHALL NOT leak past the backend adapter into stored rows, application interfaces, logs, or any user- or model-visible surface. A provider or provider-model change SHALL be registered as a new internal key rather than redefining an existing one.\n\n#### Scenario: Stored vectors reference the internal key only\n\n- **WHEN** an embedding is persisted\n- **THEN** the row identifies its model by internal key, and no provider name or provider-side model identifier is stored with it\n\n#### Scenario: Changing the provider model requires a new key\n\n- **WHEN** an operator points an existing internal key at a different provider-side model or revision\n- **THEN** the change is rejected at load with an error naming the key, rather than silently reinterpreting existing vectors\n\n### Requirement: The model that serves a corpus is selected per corpus\n\nActive-model selection SHALL be per corpus, not one global switch. Registering or enabling a model instance-wide SHALL NOT by itself change which model any corpus uses. A corpus SHALL only be marked as served by a model once that model's coverage of the corpus is complete, so that corpora backfilling at different rates cannot strand one another on missing vectors.\n\n#### Scenario: A second corpus is unaffected by another corpus's activation\n\n- **WHEN** one corpus is switched to a newly backfilled model while another corpus has only partial coverage of it\n- **THEN** the second corpus continues to be served by its own previously active model, and no query or job treats its missing vectors as an error\n\n### Requirement: Multiple model generations coexist without overwriting\n\nStorage SHALL hold at most one vector per (document, model) pair and SHALL permit vectors for different models to exist for the same document simultaneously. Vectors SHALL NOT be overwritten in place when a model changes; superseded vectors are removed only by explicit retirement after the replacement is active.\n\n#### Scenario: Backfilling a new model preserves the old one\n\n- **WHEN** a new model is registered and backfilled while an existing model already covers the corpus\n- **THEN** both models' vectors are present for the covered documents, and neither backfill destroys the other's rows\n\n#### Scenario: Retirement removes only the retired model's vectors\n\n- **WHEN** a superseded model is retired after its replacement is serving the corpus\n- **THEN** only the retired key's vectors are deleted, and the active model's coverage is unchanged\n\n### Requirement: Embedding storage enforces tenant isolation at the datastore\n\nEmbedding tables SHALL carry a denormalized owner identifier matching the projection's, with RLS `ENABLE` and `FORCE` and an owner policy over the request identity. There SHALL be **no** public-read policy: a publicly shared chat's embeddings MUST NOT be readable by any other identity, including the empty identity. Cross-tenant and public-chat negative tests SHALL run in the RLS harness alongside the projection's.\n\n#### Scenario: FORCE RLS holds against the table owner\n\n- **WHEN** the RLS harness queries embedding tables as the owning role with another user's identity set, and again with the empty identity\n- **THEN** no cross-tenant row and no public chat's row is readable\n\n#### Scenario: Embeddings are per-corpus tables\n\n- **WHEN** a second corpus gains an embedding layer\n- **THEN** it uses its own table with its own isolation policy, rather than sharing one polymorphic store whose policy would have to cover every corpus\n\n### Requirement: An embedding is valid only while its source content is unchanged\n\nEvery stored embedding SHALL record the content hash of the projection document it was derived from, and SHALL be treated as valid only while that hash still matches the document's current hash. A produced embedding whose recorded hash no longer matches SHALL be discarded and never persisted. Content that has been edited or deleted MUST NOT become recoverable through a late-arriving result.\n\n#### Scenario: Result for edited content is dropped\n\n- **WHEN** a document's content changes after its embedding was requested but before the result is persisted\n- **THEN** the result is discarded, no row is written for the superseded hash, and the document is re-queued for the current content\n\n#### Scenario: Result for deleted content restores nothing\n\n- **WHEN** a document is deleted after its embedding was requested and the result then arrives\n- **THEN** no row is created, and the deleted content is not represented anywhere in the vector layer\n\n### Requirement: Deletions propagate to the vector layer\n\nDeleting a chat, or reindexing a chat such that a document no longer exists, SHALL remove the corresponding embeddings. Embeddings MUST NOT outlive the content they were derived from beyond the freshness window.\n\n#### Scenario: Deleted chat leaves no vector residue\n\n- **WHEN** a chat is deleted\n- **THEN** no embedding row derived from any of its documents remains\n\n### Requirement: Provider access is confined to a neutral backend boundary\n\nEmbedding production SHALL go through a provider-neutral interface exposing document and query embedding operations. Provider request and response formats, endpoints, and credentials SHALL remain internal to an adapter behind that interface. A returned vector SHALL be validated for the registered dimensions and for finite values before it is persisted; a result failing validation SHALL be rejected rather than stored. Where results are correlated to requests, correlation SHALL use an explicit identifier carrying model key, document, and content hash — never response ordering.\n\n#### Scenario: A provider swap changes no consumer\n\n- **WHEN** the configured backend is changed from a self-hosted endpoint to a hosted one, or the reverse\n- **THEN** no code outside the adapter changes, and stored vectors remain addressable by their internal model key\n\n#### Scenario: A malformed vector is rejected\n\n- **WHEN** a provider returns a vector whose length differs from the registered dimensions, or containing a non-finite value\n- **THEN** the result is rejected with a logged error, nothing is persisted, and the document remains queued\n\n#### Scenario: Results are correlated explicitly, not positionally\n\n- **WHEN** a batch of results returns in a different order than requested, or partially\n- **THEN** each result is matched to its document by its explicit identifier, and any unmatched result is discarded\n\n### Requirement: Embedding production is asynchronous and never delays a turn\n\nEmbedding work SHALL be produced asynchronously, enqueued by the existing per-chat reindex path when a document's embedding is missing or stale for the corpus's active model. It MUST NOT run inside a user-facing write, MUST NOT delay turn completion, and MUST NOT fail a user-facing write or a lexical rebuild. Lexical freshness SHALL be unaffected; embedding freshness MAY lag, and a bounded lag is not an error state.\n\n#### Scenario: A turn completes at lexical speed\n\n- **WHEN** a turn finalizes on an instance with embeddings configured\n- **THEN** the turn's content is lexically searchable on that request's completion exactly as before, and the embedding work is queued rather than awaited\n\n#### Scenario: Embedding failure does not damage the lexical projection\n\n- **WHEN** the embedding backend is unreachable or returns errors for a period\n- **THEN** lexical indexing, search, and turn completion continue unaffected, and affected documents remain queued for retry\n\n### Requirement: Backfill covers the existing corpus and is idempotent\n\nThere SHALL be an operator-invocable backfill that produces embeddings for existing documents not yet covered by a model. Re-running it after completion SHALL be a no-op, determined by recorded content hashes rather than by re-embedding. Per-chat indexing and embedding progress SHALL be observable so an operator can tell coverage from lag.\n\n#### Scenario: Re-running a completed backfill costs nothing\n\n- **WHEN** the backfill is run a second time against an already-covered corpus\n- **THEN** no provider request is issued and no row is written\n\n#### Scenario: Coverage is observable\n\n- **WHEN** an operator inspects a chat during backfill\n- **THEN** its lexical indexed-at and its embedding progress are separately visible\n\n### Requirement: An unconfigured instance degrades to lexical rather than failing\n\nWith no embedding model configured, or with the configured backend unavailable, the instance SHALL start and run normally with lexical search unchanged. Absent embedding configuration SHALL NOT be an error path, SHALL NOT fail boot, and SHALL NOT emit recurring error-level noise. Every existing test and every existing search behavior SHALL pass unchanged on such an instance.\n\n#### Scenario: No embedding configuration runs clean\n\n- **WHEN** an instance boots with no embedding model configured\n- **THEN** it starts successfully, search behaves exactly as before, and no embedding job or provider request is created\n\n### Requirement: Model migration is staged and reversible\n\nReplacing the model that serves a corpus SHALL follow a staged order: register the new key, backfill it alongside the incumbent, evaluate it against the recorded relevance baseline, activate it for the corpus, and only then retire the incumbent's vectors after a safe period. Activation SHALL be reversible until retirement, and SHALL NOT be permitted while coverage is incomplete.\n\n#### Scenario: Activation is refused on incomplete coverage\n\n- **WHEN** activation is attempted for a model that has not finished backfilling the corpus\n- **THEN** the activation is refused naming the missing coverage, and the incumbent continues to serve\n\n#### Scenario: Activation is reversible before retirement\n\n- **WHEN** a newly activated model is reverted before the incumbent's vectors are retired\n- **THEN** the incumbent serves the corpus again with no re-backfill\n\n### Requirement: Credentials and configuration never enter derived content\n\nProvider credentials, endpoints, and configuration text SHALL never be written into projection content, embedding rows, logs, errors, or diagnostics. An error concerning a model or provider entry SHALL identify it by internal key and field name, never by resolved value.\n\n#### Scenario: A backend error names no secret\n\n- **WHEN** the embedding backend fails authentication\n- **THEN** the logged error names the model key and the failing field, and contains no credential, no endpoint secret, and no interpolated value\n\n### Requirement: pgvector is a declared platform dependency\n\nThe schema SHALL create the `vector` extension via a migration. Unlike `pg_trgm`, `vector` is not present in the stock Postgres image, so the development, RLS-harness, and CI database images SHALL provide it, and pgvector SHALL be documented as a self-host deployment requirement. Migrations SHALL create the extension as the non-superuser owning role without superuser intervention.\n\n#### Scenario: Fresh database provisions cleanly\n\n- **WHEN** migrations run against a fresh pgvector-capable database as the non-superuser owner role\n- **THEN** the extension, registry, and embedding schema are created without superuser intervention\n\n#### Scenario: A database without pgvector fails loudly at migration\n\n- **WHEN** migrations run against an image lacking the `vector` extension\n- **THEN** migration fails with an error naming the missing extension, rather than the application starting into a partially provisioned state\n",
