            "content": "## Context\n\nSee `proposal.md` — Why. Requirements are in `specs/`.\n\nWhat already exists and constrains this design:\n\n- `search_chat_documents` is the chunk projection: `id` (uuid PK), `owner_user_id` (text, denormalized), `chat_id`, `chunk_ordinal`, `chunker_version`, `content`, `normalized_content`, **`content_hash`** (sha256 over chunker version + presentation content + normalized content + message range), and a STORED `fts` column. Its header comment already reserves `content_hash` to \"(phase 2) guard stale embeddings\" — this design consumes exactly that.\n- RLS is `ENABLE` + `FORCE` with a single `FOR ALL` owner policy over `current_setting('app.current_user_id', true)` and **no public-read policy**; the empty identity matches nothing. All writes happen under `tenantDb.runAs(owner)`.\n- Reindex is a producer/consumer model over pg-boss: `SEARCH_REINDEX_QUEUE` (`policy: 'stately'`, `singletonKey = chatId`) drained by `search-reindex.worker.ts`, with `SEARCH_SWEEP_QUEUE` as the cross-tenant discovery producer using a `SECURITY DEFINER` function owned by the BYPASSRLS `app_rls` role that returns identifiers only.\n- The rebuild runs under **REPEATABLE READ** with retry on serialization failure, and advances `search_chat_state.indexed_at` monotonically (`GREATEST`).\n- `instance-config` is config-as-code: `providers[]` entries `{ id, type, key?, baseUrl? }` with `{env:…}`/`{path:…}` interpolation and empty-resolution-means-keyless, and `models[]` referencing `providers[].id`. Resolved credentials are redacted everywhere.\n- The Postgres image is digest-pinned in three places: `compose.yaml`, `apps/api/vitest.integration.global-setup.mts` (Testcontainers), and `playwright.config.ts` (`E2E_DB_PG_IMAGE`).\n\n## Goals / Non-Goals\n\n**Goals:**\n\n- Land vector storage, the provider boundary, and a backfilled corpus with **zero observable change** to search.\n- Keep the tenant-isolation posture identical to the lexical projection, including the FORCE-RLS-with-no-public-read shape and cross-tenant negatives.\n- Make every embedding traceable to the exact content it describes, so stale and deleted content can never resurface.\n- Make the whole layer optional: an instance with no embedding configuration must be indistinguishable from today.\n\n**Non-Goals (design-level, beyond the proposal's scope boundary):**\n\n- No vector index (HNSW/IVFFlat) is created. Nothing queries vectors in this change, so an index would be pure cost — see D4.\n- No admin UI or HTTP surface for the catalog, coverage, or activation. Operator config plus logs are the whole interface.\n- No corpus other than chat chunks, though the backend and queue contracts are placed to be reused.\n- No cost accounting, rate limiting, or provider-spend controls beyond bounded batch sizes and retry backoff.\n\n## Decisions\n\n### D1. The model catalog is config-as-code, with a database ledger only for binding integrity\n\n**Decision.** Embedding models are declared in `llame.config.json` as `embeddingModels[]`, referencing an existing `providers[].id`. The database stores a small ledger row per internal key recording the binding actually used to produce vectors (provider id, provider-side model, revision, dimensions, distance metric, prefixes). On load, a declared key whose binding differs from its ledger row is rejected, naming the key and the field that changed.\n\n**Why over the database-table registry sketched on #196.** `instance-config`'s purpose statement is explicit: operator/system settings are config-as-code, and \"tenant-owned settings are out of scope: they are database rows under RLS, never file entries.\" An embedding model is operator configuration by that rule, and the repo already moved the executable model catalog the same direction in `providers-and-models-as-code`. A DB registry would need its own migration, its own admin surface to be editable, and would fork the operator-settings story for one feature.\n\n**Why keep any DB row at all.** Vectors are meaningless without knowing what produced them. If the catalog were purely config, an operator could point an existing key at a different provider model and silently mix two embedding spaces in one column — the failure is undetectable at query time and corrupts ranking rather than erroring. The ledger makes that a boot-time rejection. It is written once per key, on first use, and never read on a hot path.\n\n**Alternatives considered.** (a) DB registry table as #196 sketched — rejected above. (b) Pure config with no ledger — rejected: silent embedding-space mixing. (c) Fold the binding fingerprint into every vector row — rejected: duplicates one fact per chunk and still needs a lookup to detect a change.\n\n### D2. Vectors live in their own table, one row per (document, model), with a dimensionless `vector` column\n\n**Decision.** `search_chat_embeddings`, PK `(document_id, model_key)`, with `document_id` FK to `search_chat_documents` `ON DELETE CASCADE`, denormalized `owner_user_id` and `chat_id`, `embedded_content_hash`, `embedding vector` (no dimension modifier), and `created_at`.\n\n**Why a separate table rather than a column on the projection.** Two writers with different cadences and different failure modes would otherwise share a row: the lexical tier rewrites content synchronously on turn completion under REPEATABLE READ, while the embedding tier writes minutes later from a queue. Separate tables make the writer partition structural instead of a convention someone has to remember — which is also why the `search-projection` delta needs no new invariant about it. Cascade from the document gives deletion propagation for free, and the projection rebuild already deletes documents whose content no longer exists.\n\n**Why dimensionless.** A dimensioned `vector(N)` column forecloses running two models of different sizes during a migration, which D6 requires. Postgres permits a dimensionless `vector` column; dimensions are then validated in application code against the catalog before insert. The cost is that a vector index cannot be built on the bare column without a per-model partial index over a cast expression — deferred with D4, and payable then.\n\n**Why denormalize owner and chat.** Same reason the projection does: the RLS policy and the in-query seatbelt filter directly on `owner_user_id` with no correlated subquery per candidate row. Denormalizing `chat_id` additionally makes per-chat delete and coverage counting a single index scan.\n\n### D3. Isolation copies the projection exactly, including the migration hand-append\n\n**Decision.** `ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` + a hand-appended `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` in the migration (Drizzle cannot express FORCE), one `FOR ALL` owner policy on `owner_user_id = current_setting('app.current_user_id', true)` with matching `WITH CHECK`, and **no public-read policy**. The embedding worker writes under `runAs(owner)` like the reindex worker. Discovery of *which* chats need embedding reuses the existing identifiers-only `SECURITY DEFINER` path rather than introducing a second cross-tenant reader.\n\n**Why.** This is the fifth table to follow the pattern (`chats`, `runs`, org units, pins, the projection). Deviating on any of the three parts — FORCE, no-public-read, `runAs` writes — is how a publicly shared chat's derived rows become readable by the empty identity. The migration exception must be recorded in the API migration exception ledger, as the projection's was.\n\n**Consequence to test explicitly.** A vector is a lossy but real encoding of content. Treating \"it's just numbers\" as less sensitive than text would be wrong: cross-tenant read of an embedding is a cross-tenant read of content. The RLS negatives cover the same two identities as the projection's — another user, and the empty identity — and are asserted for the public-chat case specifically.\n\n### D4. No vector index in this change\n\n**Decision.** Ship the table with no HNSW or IVFFlat index; add it in the retrieval change if measurement justifies it.\n\n**Why.** Nothing reads vectors here, so an index buys nothing and costs build time, write amplification on every embed, and a maintenance surface. It is also the wrong time to choose one: HNSW parameters should be tuned against real query latency on the real corpus, which only exists after this change ships and backfills. Deferring keeps the migration small and the decision informed. The dimensionless column (D2) means the eventual index is a per-model partial index over a cast expression — noted here so the follow-on change is not surprised by it.\n\n### D5. Embedding work rides the existing reindex path as a second consumer\n\n**Decision.** A new `SEARCH_EMBED_QUEUE` with `policy: 'stately'` and `singletonKey = chatId`, enqueued by the reindex worker after a rebuild commits, and by the sweep for chats whose embedding coverage lags. The embed worker resolves the chat's documents under `runAs(owner)`, embeds those whose `(document_id, model_key)` row is missing or whose `embedded_content_hash` differs, and inserts.\n\n**Why a separate queue rather than more work in the reindex job.** The two have different latency budgets, different failure modes, and different retry economics: a rebuild is local, cheap, and must converge fast; an embed call is a network round trip to a provider that may be down for an hour. Folding them means a provider outage retries — and delays — lexical rebuilds. The `search-projection` spec already anticipated this by naming \"phase-2 embedding work\" among the coalesced async paths.\n\n**Why per-chat granularity rather than per-document.** It matches the existing `singletonKey = chatId` coalescing, so a burst of writes to one chat collapses to one pending embed job exactly as it collapses to one pending rebuild. Per-document jobs would multiply queue rows by chunk count for no gain, since the provider call is batched per chat anyway.\n\n**Ordering.** Enqueue happens *after* the rebuild transaction commits, so the embed worker never reads documents that a rolled-back rebuild would have removed. A rebuild that lands while an embed job is in flight simply changes hashes, and D7 discards the stale results.\n\n### D6. The active model is per corpus, and activation is a runtime state, not a boot assertion\n\n**Decision.** Config declares the *intended* model for a corpus. The system serves it only once coverage is complete; until then the corpus stays on its incumbent (in this change: nothing, since nothing serves). Incomplete coverage is a normal, logged state — never a boot failure. Config-integrity failures (a corpus naming an undeclared model id, a dangling `provider`) *are* boot failures, because they are unambiguous operator errors.\n\n**Why not fail boot on incomplete coverage.** The intended model is declared before its backfill runs, by construction. Failing boot would make the documented migration order impossible to execute.\n\n**Why per corpus.** Knowledge/RAG and curated memory will embed later corpora at their own pace. A single global `active` flag would either strand them on missing vectors or block the chat corpus behind the slowest one.\n\n### D7. Content hash is the sole validity rule, checked at insert time\n\n**Decision.** Every row records the `content_hash` the vector was produced from. Insert is conditional on that hash still matching the document's current `content_hash`; a mismatch discards the result silently (debug-logged, not error-logged) and leaves the document to the next pass. Results are correlated to requests by an explicit key of `(model_key, document_id, content_hash)` — never by response position.\n\n**Why this and nothing else.** It collapses three separate hazards — a message edited mid-flight, a chat deleted mid-flight, and a chunker-version bump invalidating every chunk — into one comparison. The alternative (timestamps or a generation counter) reintroduces the microsecond-truncation class of bug that already bit the projection's `indexed_at`.\n\n**Why insert-time rather than fetch-time.** The gap that matters is between request and persist, which spans a network call; checking only before the call leaves the whole window open. A conditional insert closes it inside the same statement.\n\n**Why positional correlation is banned.** Providers may reorder, omit, or partially fail a batch. Position-based correlation then writes one document's vector onto another's row — a corruption with no error and no detectable symptom until ranking silently degrades.\n\n### D8. One synchronous OpenAI-compatible adapter; no batch API\n\n**Decision.** Ship `EmbeddingBackend` with `embedQuery` and `embedDocuments`, and exactly one adapter speaking the OpenAI-compatible `/embeddings` endpoint. That single adapter covers hosted OpenAI, Ollama, and every OpenAI-compatible gateway, reusing the existing `providers[]` connection.\n\n**Why no batch adapter now.** The asynchronous batch API halves cost at the price of a submit/poll state machine, durable batch records, and a whole second class of partial-failure handling. Backfilling a personal-scale corpus does not need it, and the interface reserves the shape (`submitDocumentBatch` / `getDocumentBatch`) so adding it later is additive. Building it now is speculative machinery for a cost that may never bind.\n\n**Why `embedQuery` ships despite nothing calling it.** It is the half of the interface the follow-on retrieval change consumes, and the asymmetric-prefix contract (D1) is only meaningful if both sides exist. It is exercised by unit tests only.\n\n### D9. The pgvector image swap is one deliberate deployment change\n\n**Decision.** Move all three pinned image references to a digest-pinned `pgvector/pgvector:pg17`, document pgvector as a self-host requirement in README and `apps/api/AGENTS.md`, and do it in the first task of the change so every subsequent task runs against the real target.\n\n**Why not make it optional.** A conditional extension means two schema shapes, two migration paths, and a permanently forked test matrix — for a self-hosted product whose operator already runs a Postgres container and can change one image tag. Optionality here is a maintenance liability, not a kindness. The *feature* stays optional (no configured model ⇒ lexical), which is the optionality that actually matters to a user.\n\n**Why pgvector/pgvector:pg17 over building the extension into a custom image.** It is the upstream-maintained image tracking the same Postgres major already pinned; a custom image is a build to maintain for no gain.\n\n## Risks / Trade-offs\n\n- **Self-hosters cannot upgrade without changing their database image** → Called out as BREAKING in the proposal, documented in README and AGENTS in the same PR, and stated in the CHANGELOG entry. The migration fails loudly on a non-pgvector database rather than starting into a half-provisioned state.\n- **A dimensionless vector column defers, but does not remove, the index problem** → D4 records that the eventual index is a per-model partial index over a cast; the retrieval change owns choosing and tuning it against real latency.\n- **Embedding an existing corpus is real provider spend, proportional to chunk count** → Backfill is operator-invoked rather than automatic on deploy, is idempotent by hash, and a self-hosted backend makes it free. Coverage is observable so an operator can stop and resume.\n- **A provider outage silently stops embedding progress** → Coverage lag is observable per chat, the queue retries with backoff, and lexical search is unaffected by construction (D5). Degradation is a lag, never an error path for the user.\n- **Two models coexisting doubles vector storage for the duration of a migration** → Bounded and temporary by D6's staged order; retirement deletes the incumbent's rows once the replacement serves.\n- **The ledger can reject boot after an operator edits a live key** → That is the intended behavior, and the error names the key and the changed field. The documented remedy is to register a new key, which is also the correct migration path.\n- **Vectors are content** → D3's negatives assert the public-chat and cross-tenant cases explicitly, so \"it's just numbers\" never becomes an implicit isolation exemption.\n\n## Migration Plan\n\n1. Swap the three pinned Postgres images to digest-pinned pgvector; confirm the existing suites pass unchanged on the new image before adding anything.\n2. Ship the migration (extension, ledger, embedding table, FORCE RLS hand-append) and the RLS negatives. At this point the schema exists and nothing writes to it.\n3. Ship the config surface and the backend adapter. An instance with no `embeddingModels` is unchanged and no queue is created.\n4. Ship the queue, worker, and the reindex enqueue hook. New turns begin embedding on instances that configured a model.\n5. Run backfill for the existing corpus, observing coverage.\n6. Re-run the relevance eval and confirm `BASELINE.md` is unchanged — this change must not move a single metric.\n\n**Rollback.** Steps 3–5 roll back by removing `embeddingModels` from config: the queue stops producing, existing vectors become inert, and search is unaffected. Step 2 rolls back by a migration reverting the table; the image swap in step 1 is forward-only in practice, since reverting it would break any instance that already ran the migration.\n\n## Open Questions\n\n- **Chunk-level versus turn-level embedding granularity.** This design embeds the existing chunks, since they are what the projection already produces and what retrieval will rank. Whether a coarser or finer unit retrieves better is a question for the retrieval change, answerable by re-embedding without a schema change.\n- **Which model to ship as the documented default.** A bge-m3-class multilingual model is the intended recommendation for the self-hosted path, but the specific choice should be made against the extended eval dataset — including inflected Russian and Spanish queries — rather than asserted here. Nothing in this design depends on the answer.\n",
