he inline Tier-1 rebuild at finalize, which runs no queue job at all — the reindex worker only runs on the Tier-1 fallback, on fork, and on sweep-discovered chats. Enqueueing only from the worker would mean an ordinary turn on a configured instance produces **no** embedding work until a sweep noticed it. A queue send is not inline indexing, so this adds nothing to the projection's synchronous path and conflicts with none of its requirements; the `stately` + `singletonKey` coalescing already absorbs the extra sends.\n\n**Why per-chat granularity rather than per-document.**\"\"\"\nassert old in s; s=s.replace(old,new,1)\n\n# D10 new decision\nold=\"\"\"## Risks / Trade-offs\"\"\"\nnew=\"\"\"### D10. Embedding lag needs its own discovery predicate and its own provisioning check\n\n**Decision.** Add a second identifiers-only `SECURITY DEFINER` function owned by the BYPASSRLS `app_rls` role whose predicate is **embedding coverage** — documents with no row for the intended model key, or with a row whose `embedded_content_hash` no longer matches. Extend `db:provision-rls` to assign its ownership, and extend the existing boot self-check to verify it, with the same loud-error-non-fatal behavior.\n\n**Why a second function rather than reusing the lexical one.** `llame_search_stale_chats` compares `chats.updated_at` against `search_chat_state.indexed_at`. A corpus that is fully indexed and never embedded is not stale by that predicate, so it returns **zero rows** — the backfill would have no enumerator and the sweep would never see embedding lag. Widening the existing function instead would couple two independently-evolving predicates and change a shipped contract that phase 1's tests pin.\n\n**Why the provisioning check is not optional.** This is the exact failure that already bit phase 1: an unprovisioned `SECURITY DEFINER` function under FORCE RLS returns zero rows *without error*, which is indistinguishable from \"everything is covered.\" Leo's own instance had 7 of 8 chat