ath, not just the reindex worker (D5).** The happy path for an ordinary turn is the inline Tier-1 finalize rebuild, which runs no queue job — the reindex worker only runs on the Tier-1 fallback, on fork, and on sweep-discovered chats. Enqueueing only from the worker would mean a normal turn on a configured instance produces no embedding work until a sweep noticed it.\n\n**4. Bulk work is explicit; only incremental lag is automatic (D6).** Two kinds of lag were being conflated. New or edited content under an already-serving model is bounded by write volume and stays automatic. A newly declared model with zero coverage, or an `embed_input_version` bump, is unbounded and proportional to corpus size — that is advanced **only** by an operator-invoked `backfill`. A one-line config edit must never start corpus-scale provider spend or hours of self-hosted saturation. Consequently the sweep maintains only models already serving a corpus. Three model-change cases are now specified: redefining an in-use key is rejected at boot, switching a corpus's intended model is allowed and inert until covered, and undeclaring a model with vectors leaves them inert and prunable — never auto-deleted.\n\nAlso settled, where this issue left options open:\n\n- **The OpenAI Batch adapter is not built** (D8). One synchronous OpenAI-compatible adapter covers hosted OpenAI, Ollama, and every compatible gateway. The interface reserves the batch shape so it stays additive.\n- **No vector index in this change** (D4). Nothing reads vectors here, so HNSW would be pure cost — and its parameters should be tuned against real query latency on a real corpus, which only exists after this ships. The dimensionless `vector` column (D2) means the eventual index is a per-model partial index over a cast; noted so #197 is not surprised.\n- **Cosine, vectors stored exactly as the provider returns them** (D12). No normalization in the adapter: that would be a silent lossy rewrite the ledger cannot detect.\n- **Embedding input is the document's `content` verbatim, role labels included** (D11). An earlier draft stripped them by analogy with the projection's rule that role labels must not affect lexical ranking; that rationale does not transfer, because the lexical hazard is literal token collision and embeddings have no token-level matching. Confidence is moderate and the question is handed to #197 as an A/B.\n- **Off by default, config-file only.** No environment variable, no database setting, no per-user or per-tenant control.\n\nTwo dependencies discovered during grilling and split out:\n\n- **#517** — `chunkByCharBudget` always takes at least one item, so a message larger than `CHUNK_MAX_CHARS` is emitted as one oversized document. Harmless lexically, unembeddable in practice. Fixing it now costs one projection rebuild; fixing it after this ships costs a rebuild **and** a full re-embed.\n- **#518** — model-generated chunk context, deliberately deferred behind #517's deterministic anchor so the lift is measured rather than assumed.\n\nOpen question 1 from the design (chunk-level vs turn-level granularity) is **closed by the codebase**: `CHUNK_MAX_CHARS = 3000; // ≈750 tokens — inside phase-2 embedding budgets`, grill-locked in phase 1. The chunk shape was already sized for this work.\nEOF\ngh issue comment 196 --body-file $S/c196.md 2>&1 | tail -2\ncat > $S/c197.md <<'EOF'\nTwo things this phase inherits from the `add-chat-search-embeddings` design, recorded now so they are not rediscovered later.\n\n**1. Role labels in embedding input are an open A/B for this phase.** #196 embeds the document's `content` verbatim, role markers included (D11). That call is moderate-confidence mechanical reasoning, not evidence: constant prefixes demonstrably move embeddings (the whole E5/BGE `query:`/`passage:` mechanism works that way), and a constant token in every document adds a shared component that compresses score dynamic range under mean pooling — but the query lacks the labels too, so